      "id": "5ea64250-e33c-43eb-aadd-b1f2b428618f",
      "title": "Where is the â€œChristâ€ in â€œChristianity?â€",
      "author": "Quran & Science",
      "content": "Religious scholars have long attributed the tenets of Christian faith more to Paulâ€™s teachings than to those of Jesus.Â  But as much as I would like to jump into that subject, I think it best to back up and take a quick, speculative look at the Old Testament.\n\nThe Old Testament teaches that Jacob wrestled with God.Â  In fact, the Old Testament records that Jacob not only wrestled with God, but that Jacob prevailed (Genesis 32:24-30).Â  Now, bear in mind, weâ€™re talking about a tiny blob of protoplasm wrestling the Creator of a universe 240,000,000,000,000,000,000,000 miles in diameter, containing over a billion galaxies of which oursâ€”the Milky Way Galaxyâ€”is just one (and a small one, at that), and prevailing? Â Iâ€™m sorry, but someone was a couple pages short of a codex when they scribed that passage.Â  The point is, however, that this passage leaves us in a quandary.Â  We either have to question the Jewish concept of God or accept their explanation that â€œGodâ€ does not mean â€œGodâ€ in the above verses, but rather it means either an angel or a man (which, in essence, means the Old Testament is not to be trusted).Â  In fact, this textual difficulty has become so problematic that more recent Bibles have tried to cover it up by changing the translation from â€œGodâ€ to â€œman.â€ Â What they cannot change, however, is the foundational scripture from which the Jewish Bible is translated, and this continues to read â€œGod.â€\n\nUnreliability is a recurring problem in the Old Testament, the most prominent example being the confusion between God and Satan! Â II Samuel 24:1 reads:\n\nâ€œAgain the anger of the LORD was aroused against Israel, and He moved David against them to say, â€˜Go, number Israel and Judah.â€™â€\n\nHowever, I Chronicles 21:1 states: â€œNow Satan stood up against Israel, and moved David to number Israel.â€\n\nUhhh, which was it? Â The Lord, or Satan? Â Both verses describe the same event in history, but one speaks of God and the other of Satan.Â  There is a slight (like, total) difference.\n\nChristians would like to believe that the New Testament is free of such difficulties, but they are sadly deceived.Â  In fact, there are so many contradictions that authors have devoted books to this subject.Â  For example, Matthew 2:14 and Luke 2:39 differ over whether Jesusâ€™ family fled to Egypt or Nazareth.Â  Matthew 6:9-13 and Luke 11:2-4 differ over the wording of the â€œLordâ€™s Prayer.â€ Â Matthew 11:13-14, 17:11-13 and John 1:21 disagree over whether or not John the Baptist was Elijah.\n\nThings get worse when we enter the arena of the alleged crucifixion: Who carried the crossâ€”Simon (Luke 23:26, Matthew 27:32, Mark 15:21) or Jesus (John 19:17)? Â Was Jesus dressed in a scarlet robe (Matthew 27:28) or a purple robe (John 19:2)? Â Did the Roman soldiers put gall (Matthew 27:34) or myrrh (Mark 15:23) in his wine? Â Was Jesus crucified before the third hour (Mark 15:25) or after the sixth hour (John 19:14-15)? Â Did Jesus ascend the first day (Luke 23:43) or not (John 20:17)? Â Were Jesusâ€™ last words, â€œFather, â€˜into Your hands I commit my spiritâ€™â€ (Luke 23:46), or were they â€œIt is finishedâ€ (John 19:30)?\n\nThese are only a few of a long list of scriptural inconsistencies, and they underscore the difficulty in trusting the New Testament as scripture.Â  Nonetheless, there are those who do trust their salvation to the New Testament, and it is these Christians who need to answer the question, â€œWhere is the â€˜Christâ€™ in â€˜Christianity?â€™Â  â€œThis, in fact, is a supremely fair question.Â  On one hand we have a religion named after Jesus Christ, but on the other hand the tenets of orthodox Christianity, which is to say Trinitarian Christianity, contradict virtually everything he taught.\n\nI know, I knowâ€”those of you who arenâ€™t screaming â€œHeretic!â€ Â are gathering firewood and planting a stake.Â  But wait.Â  Put down the high-powered rifle and listen.Â  Trinitarian Christianity claims to base its doctrines on a combination of Jesusâ€™ and Paulâ€™s teachings.Â  The problem is, these teachings are anything but complementary.Â  In fact, they contradict one another.\n\nTake some examples: Jesus taught Old Testament Law; Paul negated it.Â  Jesus preached orthodox Jewish creed; Paul preached mysteries of faith.Â  Jesus spoke of accountability; Paul proposed justification by faith.Â  Jesus described himself as an ethnic prophet; Paul defined him as a universal prophet.[1] Jesus taught prayer to God, Paul set Jesus up as intercessor.Â  Jesus taught divine unity, Pauline theologians constructed the Trinity.\n\nFor these reasons, many scholars consider Paul the main corrupter of Apostolic Christianity and Jesusâ€™ teachings.Â  Many early Christian sects held this view as well, including the second-century Christian sects known as â€œadoptionistsâ€â€“ â€œIn particular, they considered Paul, one of the most prominent authors of our New Testament, to be an arch-heretic rather than an apostle.â€[2]\n\nLehmann contributes:\n\nâ€œWhat Paul proclaimed as â€˜Christianityâ€™ was sheer heresy which could not be based on the Jewish or Essene faith, or on the teaching of Rabbi Jesus.Â  But, as Schonfield says, â€˜The Pauline heresy became the foundation of Christian orthodoxy and the legitimate church was disowned as heretical.â€™ â€¦ Paul did something that Rabbi Jesus never did and refused to do.Â  He extended Godâ€™s promise of salvation to the Gentiles; he abolished the law of Moses, and he prevented direct access to God by introducing an intermediary.â€[3]\n\nBart D. Ehrman, perhaps the most authoritative living scholar of textual criticism, comments:\n\nâ€œPaulâ€™s view was not universally accepted or, one might argue, even widely accepted â€¦. Even more striking, Paulâ€™s own letters indicate that there were outspoken, sincere, and active Christian leaders who vehemently disagreed with him on this score and considered Paulâ€™s views to be a corruption of the true message of Christ â€¦.Â  One should always bear in mind that in this very letter of Galatians Paul indicates that he confronted Peter over just such issues (Gal. 2:11-14).Â  He disagreed, that is, even with Jesusâ€™ closest disciple on the matter.â€[4]\n\nCommenting on the views of some early Christians in the Pseudo-Clementine literature, Ehrman wrote:\n\nâ€œPaul has corrupted the true faith based on a brief vision, which he has doubtless misconstrued.Â  Paul is thus the enemy of the apostles, not the chief of them.Â  He is outside the true faith, a heretic to be banned, not an apostle to be followed.â€[5]\n\nOthers elevate Paul to sainthood.Â  Joel Carmichael very clearly is not one of them:\n\nâ€œWe are a universe away from Jesus.Â  If Jesus came â€œonly to fulfillâ€ the Law and the Prophets; If he thought that â€œnot an iota, not a dotâ€ would â€œpass from the Law,â€ that the cardinal commandment was â€œHear, O Israel, the Lord Our God, the Lord is one,â€ and that â€œno one was good but Godâ€â€¦.What would he have thought of Paulâ€™s handiwork! Paulâ€™s triumph meant the final obliteration of the historic Jesus; he comes to us embalmed in Christianity like a fly in amber.â€[6]\n\nDr. Johannes Weiss contributes:\n\nâ€œHence the faith in Christ as held by the primitive churches and by Paul was something new in comparison with the preaching of Jesus; it was a new type of religion.â€[7]\n\nA new type of religion, indeed. Â And hence the question, â€œWhere is the â€˜Christâ€™ in â€˜Christianity?â€™Â  â€œIf Christianity is the religion of Jesus Christ, where are the Old Testament laws and strict monotheism of the Rabbi Jesusâ€™ Orthodox Judaism? Â Why does Christianity teach that Jesus is the son of God when Jesus called himself the â€œson of Manâ€ eighty-eight times, and not once the â€œson of God?â€ Â Why does Christianity endorse confession to priests and prayers to saints, Mary and Jesus when Jesus taught his followers:\n\nâ€œIn this manner, therefore, pray: â€˜Our Father â€¦â€™â€ (Matthew 6:9)?\n\nAnd who appointed a pope? Â Certainly not Jesus. Â True, he may have called Peter the rock upon which he would build his church (Matthew 16:18-19).Â  However, a scant five verses later, he called Peter â€œSatanâ€ and â€œan offense.â€ Â And let us not forget that this â€œrockâ€ thrice denied Jesus after Jesusâ€™ arrestâ€”poor testimony of Peterâ€™s commitment to the new church.\n\nIs it possible that Christians have been denying Jesus ever since? Â Transforming Jesusâ€™ strict monotheism to the Pauline theologiansâ€™ Trinity, replacing Rabbi Jesusâ€™ Old Testament law with Paulâ€™s â€œjustification by faith,â€ substituting the concept of Jesus having atoned for the sins of mankind for the direct accountability Jesus taught, discarding Jesusâ€™ claim to humanity for Paulâ€™s concept of Jesus having been divine, we have to question in exactly what manner Christianity respects the teachings of its prophet.\n\nA parallel issue is to define which religion does respect Jesusâ€™ teachings.Â  So letâ€™s see: Which religion honors Jesus Christ as a prophet but a man? Â Which religion adheres to strict monotheism, Godâ€™s laws, and the concept of direct accountability to God? Â Which religion denies intermediaries between man and God?\n\nIf you answered, â€œIslam,â€ you would be right.Â  And in this manner, we find the teachings of Jesus Christ better exemplified in the religion of Islam than in Christianity.Â  This suggestion, however, is not meant to be a conclusion, but rather an introduction.Â  Those who find their interest peaked by the above discussion need to take the issue seriously, open their minds and then â€¦ read on!\n\nCopyright Â© 2007 Laurence B. Brown.\n\nAbout the author: Laurence B. Brown, MD, can be contacted at BrownL38@yahoo.com. Â He is the author of The First and Final Commandment (Amana Publications) and Bearing True Witness (Dar-us-Salam). Â Forthcoming books are a historical thriller, The Eighth Scroll, and a second edition of The First and Final Commandment, rewritten and divided into MisGodâ€™ed and its sequel, Godâ€™ed\n\n[1] Jesus Christ was one more prophet in the long line of prophets sent to the astray Israelites. As he so clearly affirmed, â€œI was not sent except to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.â€ (Matthew 15:24) When Jesus sent the disciples out in the path of God, he instructed them, â€œDo not go into the way of the Gentiles, and do not enter a city of the Samaritans. But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.â€ (Matthew 10:5-6) Throughout his ministry, Jesus was never recorded as having converted a Gentile, and in fact is recorded as having initially rebuked a Gentile for seeking his favors, likening her to a dog (Matthew 15:22-28 and Mark 7:25-30). Jesus was himself a Jew, his disciples were Jews, and both he and they directed their ministries to the Jews. One wonders what this means to us now, for most of those who have taken Jesus as their â€˜personal saviorâ€™ are Gentiles, and not of the â€œlost sheep of the house of Israelâ€ to whom he was sent.\n\n[2] Ehrman, Bart D. The New Testament: A Historical Introduction to the Early Christian Writings. 2004. Oxford University Press. P. 3.\n\n[3] Lehmann, Johannes. 1972. The Jesus Report. Translated by Michael Heron. London: Souvenir Press. pp. 128, 134.\n\n[4] Ehrman, Bart D. 2003. Lost Christianities. Oxford University Press. Pp. 97-98.\n\n[5] Ehrman, Bart D. 2003. Lost Christianities. Oxford University Press. P. 184.\n\n[6] Carmichael, Joel, M.A. 1962. The Death of Jesus. New York: The Macmillan Company. p. 270.\n\n[7] Weiss, Johannes. 1909. Paul and Jesus. (Translated by Rev. H. J. Chaytor). London and New York: Harper and Brothers. p. 130.",
