      "id": "e0565795-0355-4477-84f2-912795046673",
      "title": "The Seven Earths",
      "author": "Quran & Science",
      "content": "The Sunnah of Prophet Muhammad is the second revealed source of Islam.Â  Like the Quran, it contains scientific information unavailable 1400 years ago.Â  From these miracles is the â€œsevenâ€ earths, mentioned by the Prophet in several of his sayings.\n\nFrom them are the following two:\n\nIt was narrated on the authority of Abu Salamah that a dispute arose between him and some other people (about a piece of land).Â  When he told Aisha (the Prophetâ€™s wife) about it, she said, â€˜O Abu Salamah! Â Avoid taking the land unjustly, for the Prophet said:\n\nâ€œWhoever usurps even one span of land of somebody, its depth through the seven earths will be collared to his neck.â€ (Saheeh Al-Bukhari, â€˜Book of Oppression.â€™)\n\nSalim narrated on the authority of his father that the Prophet said:\n\nâ€œWhoever takes a piece of land of others unjustly, he will sink down the seven earths on the Day of Resurrection.â€ (Saheeh Al-Bukhari, â€˜Book of Oppression.â€™)\n\nThe aforementioned hadith prohibits oppression in general, especially the taking of a piece of land belonging to others unjustly.Â  What might the seven earths refer to?\n\nStudies in geology have proven that the earth is composed of seven zones, identified from the inner to the outer layers as follows:\n\n(1)Â  The Solid Inner Core of Earth: 1.7% of the Earthâ€™s mass; depth of 5,150 â€“ 6,370 kilometers (3,219 â€“ 3,981 miles)\n\nThe inner core is solid and unattached to the mantle, suspended in the molten outer core.Â  It is believed to have solidified as a result of pressure-freezing which occurs to most liquids when temperature decreases or pressure increases.\n\n(2)Â  The Liquid Outer core: 30.8% of Earthâ€™s mass; depth of 2,890 â€“ 5,150 kilometers (1,806 â€“ 3,219 miles)\n\nThe outer core is a hot, electrically conducting liquid within which convective motion occurs.Â  This conductive layer combines with Earthâ€™s rotation to create a dynamo effect that maintains a system of electrical currents known as the Earthâ€™s magnetic field.Â  It is also responsible for the subtle jerking of Earthâ€™s rotation.Â  This layer is not as dense as pure molten iron, which indicates the presence of lighter elements.Â  Scientists suspect that about 10% of the layer is composed of sulfur and/or oxygen because these elements are abundant in the cosmos and dissolve readily in molten iron.\n\n(3)Â  The â€œDâ€ Layer: 3% of Earthâ€™s mass; depth of 2,700 â€“ 2,890 kilometers (1,688 â€“ 1,806 miles)\n\nThis layer is 200 to 300 kilometers (125 to 188 miles) thick and represents about 4% of the mantle-crust mass.Â  Although it is often identified as part of the lower mantle, seismic discontinuities suggest the â€œDâ€ layer might differ chemically from the lower mantle lying above it.Â  Scientists theorize that the material either dissolved in the core, or was able to sink through the mantle but not into the core because of its density.\n\n(4)Â  Lower Mantle: 49.2% of Earthâ€™s mass; depth of 650 â€“ 2,890 kilometers (406 -1,806 miles)\n\nThe lower mantle contains 72.9% of the mantle-crust mass and is probably composed mainly of silicon, magnesium, and oxygen.Â  It probably also contains some iron, calcium, and aluminum.Â  Scientists make these deductions by assuming the Earth has a similar abundance and proportion of cosmic elements as found in the Sun and primitive meteorites.\n\n(5)Â  Middle Mantle (Transition region): 7.5% of Earthâ€™s mass; depth of 400 â€“ 650 kilometers (250-406 miles)\n\nThe transition region or mesosphere (for middle mantle), sometimes called the fertile layer, contains 11.1% of the mantle-crust mass and is the source of basaltic magmas.Â  It also contains calcium, aluminum, and garnet, which is a complex aluminum-bearing silicate mineral.Â  This layer is dense when cold because of the garnet.Â  It is buoyant when hot because these minerals melt easily to form basalt which can then rise through the upper layers as magma.\n\n(6)Â  Upper Mantle: 10.3% of Earthâ€™s mass; depth of 10 â€“ 400 kilometers (6 â€“ 250 miles)\n\nThe upper mantle contains 15.3% of the mantle-crust mass.Â  Fragments have been excavated for our observation by eroded mountain belts and volcanic eruptions.Â  Olivine (Mg,Fe)2SiO4 and pyroxene (Mg,Fe)SiO3 have been the primary minerals found in this way.Â  These and other minerals are refractory and crystalline at high temperatures; therefore, most settle out of rising magma, either forming new material or never leaving the mantle.Â  Part of the upper mantle called the asthenosphere might be partially molten.\n\n(7)Â  Lithosphere\n\nOceanic crust: 0.099% of Earthâ€™s mass; depth of 0-10 kilometers (0 â€“ 6 miles)\n\nThe rigid, outermost layer of the Earth comprising the crust and upper mantle is called the lithosphere.Â  The oceanic crust contains 0.147% of the mantle-crust mass.Â  The majority of the Earthâ€™s crust was made through volcanic activity.Â  The oceanic ridge system, a 40,000-kilometer (25,000 mile) network of volcanoes, generates new oceanic crust at the rate of 17 km3 per year, covering the ocean floor with basalt.Â  Hawaii and Iceland are two examples of the accumulation of basalt piles.\n\nThe continental crust contains 0.554% of the mantle-crust mass.Â  This is the outer part of the Earth composed essentially of crystalline rocks.Â  These are low-density buoyant minerals dominated mostly by quartz (SiO2) and feldspars (metal-poor silicates).Â  The crust (both oceanic and continental) is the surface of the Earth; as such, it is the coldest part of our planet.Â  Because cold rocks deform slowly, we refer to this rigid outer shell as the lithosphere (the rocky or strong layer).\n\nThe layers of the earth coincide with the above mentioned hadith of the Prophet.Â  The miracle is in two matters:\n\n(1)Â  The expression of the hadith, â€˜He will sink down the seven earths on the Day of Resurrection,â€™ indicates the stratification of these â€œearthsâ€ around one center.\n\n(2)Â  The accuracy with which the Prophet of Islam referred to the seven inner layers of earth.\n\nThe only way for a desert dweller to have known these facts 1400 years ago is through revelation from God.\n\nBeatty, J. K. and A. Chaikin, eds.Â  The New Solar System.Â  Massachusetts: Sky Publishing, 3rd Edition, 1990.\n\nPress, Frank and Raymond Siever.Â  Earth.Â  New York: W. H. Freeman and Company, 1986.\n\nSeeds, Michael A. Horizons. Belmont, California: Wadsworth, 1995.\n\nEl-Najjar, Zaghloul. Â Treasures In The Sunnah: A Scientific Approach: Cairo, Al-Falah Foundation, 2004.",
      "source": "Quran and Science",
